<!-- ── §01 The Non-Determinism Gap ─────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-gap" data-code="gap">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Non-Determinism Gap</div>
<h2>Why Traditional CI/CD Breaks for LLMs</h2>

<p>Every software engineer knows the CI/CD loop: commit code, run tests, check if assertions pass, ship. It works because software is deterministic — given the same input, a function returns the same output, every time, forever.</p>

<p>LLMs shatter that assumption. Ask GPT-4 "What is the capital of France?" and you'll get: "Paris." then "The capital of France is Paris." then "It's Paris, of course." — all semantically correct, all likely to fail a <code>assert output == "Paris"</code> test. The model's temperature, sampling strategy, and even server-side load balancing mean identical prompts produce different strings.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">determinism_gap.py — same prompt, same "correct" answer, 4 different strings</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Run to see 4 samples</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="gap-canvas" height="220" style="display:block;width:100%;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.625rem;margin-top:1rem;align-items:center">
      <button id="gap-run-btn" style="padding:.3rem .875rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2)">▶ Run eval</button>
      <span id="gap-result-text" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Showing 4 LLM samples for the same prompt</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The deeper problem: even LLM-specific string checks like <em>does the response contain "Paris"</em> break down in real tasks. For summarization, two correct summaries can share no words. For code generation, two correct programs can look completely different. For customer support, "I can help with that" and "Of course! Let me assist you" are both perfect but nothing alike.</p>

<p>What you actually need is a way to measure <strong>semantic quality</strong> — not string equality. That's the core shift CI/CD for LLMs demands.</p>

<div class="callout callout-amber">
  <div class="callout-title">⚠ The Regression Trap</div>
  <p>Teams often discover this the hard way: a prompt change that makes the app feel noticeably better causes 60% of their string-match tests to "fail" — while a prompt change that makes it worse passes all tests because the output format is unchanged. String-match tests measure the <em>wrong thing</em>.</p>
</div>

<h3>What changes about the pipeline</h3>
<p>Traditional CI/CD: <strong>assert exact output</strong>. LLM CI/CD: <strong>evaluate semantic quality</strong>. The machinery changes too — instead of a test suite that runs in milliseconds, you need:</p>
<ul>
  <li>A <strong>golden dataset</strong> of representative inputs with expected <em>behaviors</em> (not strings)</li>
  <li><strong>Evaluation functions</strong> that score semantic quality rather than comparing strings</li>
  <li><strong>Baselines and thresholds</strong> — "new version must score ≥ baseline on faithfulness"</li>
  <li>A way to gate deploys on quality scores, not just whether tests pass or fail</li>
</ul>
</section>

<!-- ── §02 The Eval Triangle ─────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-pyramid" data-code="pyramid">
<div class="section-no">02 — The Eval Triangle</div>
<h2>Three Tiers of Testing, Matched to Cost and Frequency</h2>

<p>The classic software testing pyramid — unit tests at the base, integration in the middle, E2E at the top — translates naturally to LLM apps, but the mapping isn't one-to-one. Speed and semantic depth pull in opposite directions. Here's how the tiers shake out:</p>

<!-- Tier 1 -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Eval Tier Navigator — step through the pyramid</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">use Prev / Next</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="tier-list">

      <div class="step-box active" id="tier-0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="tier-pills">
            <span class="tier-pill tp-fast">⚡ &lt; 1 second</span>
            <span class="tier-pill tp-fast">🟢 free</span>
            <span class="tier-pill tp-fast">every commit</span>
          </div>
          <div class="step-title">Tier 1 — Assertion Evals (Unit Tests)</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Deterministic, code-based checks that run instantly. No LLM calls required. These catch structural failures, format violations, and obvious regressions immediately.</div>
          <div class="step-code">assert "error" not in response.lower()
assert len(response) &gt; 20
assert json.loads(response)["action"] in VALID_ACTIONS
assert response_time_ms &lt; 3000</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="step-box" id="tier-1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="tier-pills">
            <span class="tier-pill tp-mid">⏱ 2–10 min</span>
            <span class="tier-pill tp-mid">🟡 ~$0.01–0.10 per run</span>
            <span class="tier-pill tp-mid">every PR</span>
          </div>
          <div class="step-title">Tier 2 — LLM-as-Judge Evals</div>
          <div class="step-desc">A strong model (Claude, GPT-4) evaluates each output against quality criteria. Catches hallucinations, coherence failures, and safety issues. Run on a golden dataset of 50–200 representative examples.</div>
          <div class="step-code">score = judge_llm.evaluate(
    input=test_prompt,
    output=app_response,
    criteria=["faithfulness", "helpfulness", "safety"]
)
assert score.faithfulness &gt;= 0.85</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="step-box" id="tier-2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="tier-pills">
            <span class="tier-pill tp-slow">🕐 hours–days</span>
            <span class="tier-pill tp-slow">🔴 real users</span>
            <span class="tier-pill tp-slow">major releases</span>
          </div>
          <div class="step-title">Tier 3 — Shadow Deploy &amp; A/B Testing</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Run old and new versions in parallel. Route a fraction of real traffic to the new version. Measure actual user outcomes — click-through, task completion, session length. The ground truth, but expensive and slow.</div>
          <div class="step-code">router.shadow_deploy(
    baseline=v1_model,
    candidate=v2_model,
    traffic_fraction=0.10,
    duration_hours=48,
    success_metric="task_completion_rate"
)</div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="tier-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <button id="tier-next">Next →</button>
      <span class="step-prog" id="tier-prog">Tier 1 of 3</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The practical rhythm: Tier 1 runs on every <code>git commit</code> (fast, free, always). Tier 2 runs on every pull request (a few minutes, small API cost). Tier 3 runs only for significant model or prompt changes (real user exposure, high stakes).</p>

<div class="callout callout-green">
  <div class="callout-title">✓ The 5-Metric Rule</div>
  <p>Don't track 20 metrics. Pick 1–2 use-case-specific metrics (e.g. "booking success rate" for a travel agent) plus 2–3 architecture-specific ones (e.g. "faithfulness" and "context recall" for a RAG system). Five metrics, all tracked, beats twenty metrics you ignore.</p>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ── §03 LLM-as-Judge ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-judge" data-code="judge">
<div class="section-no">03 — LLM-as-Judge</div>
<h2>Automating Semantic Quality Assessment</h2>

<p>The workhorse of LLM CI/CD is LLM-as-judge: using a strong LLM to evaluate another LLM's output. The idea was validated by Zheng et al. (2023) on MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena — GPT-4 as judge achieves <strong>&gt;80% agreement with human raters</strong>, matching the human-to-human agreement rate. That's good enough to gate deploys on.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">LLM-as-Judge architecture — data flow through the eval pipeline</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click on a node to highlight the data at that stage</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <svg id="judge-svg" viewBox="0 0 720 300" style="width:100%;display:block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>
    <div id="judge-detail" style="margin-top:.875rem;min-height:52px;font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted2);font-family:var(--ff-mono);line-height:1.6;background:var(--card2);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:6px;padding:.65rem .875rem;transition:all .2s"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>The three failure modes of LLM judges</h3>
<p>LLM judges are powerful but not perfect. Three well-documented biases can corrupt your evals if left unaddressed:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Position bias</strong> — judges prefer the first response in a pair comparison. Fix: swap order and check for consistency; only count a winner if both orderings agree.</li>
  <li><strong>Verbosity bias</strong> — longer responses score higher independent of quality. Fix: normalize for length; penalize padding. Or use single-response scoring instead of pairwise.</li>
  <li><strong>Self-enhancement bias</strong> — a model gives itself inflated scores. Fix: never use the same model family as judge and as evaluated. Use a different provider if possible.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Eval prompt structure</h3>
<p>The judge prompt is as important as the judge model. Well-structured judge prompts:</p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">Judge Prompt Template</div>
You are evaluating an AI assistant's response.
<span class="math-var">Criteria</span>: [faithfulness | helpfulness | safety | ...]

<span class="math-var">Question</span>: &#123;user_question&#125;
<span class="math-var">Context</span>: &#123;retrieved_context&#125;        <span class="math-comment">← for RAG systems</span>
<span class="math-var">Response</span>: &#123;model_output&#125;

Score on a scale 1–5 where:
  5 = excellent, no issues
  3 = acceptable, minor issues
  1 = unacceptable, major issues

Reason step-by-step before scoring. End with: Score: X
</div>

<p>The chain-of-thought reasoning step before the score ("reason step-by-step") dramatically improves reliability — it forces the judge to articulate what's wrong before committing to a number, preventing gut-feel scoring.</p>

<div class="callout callout-purple">
  <div class="callout-title">💡 Calibrate Against Humans First</div>
  <p>Before trusting your LLM judge, annotate 50–100 examples by hand and measure precision/recall against the judge's decisions. A judge with 90% accuracy might have 60% precision on "fail" verdicts — too many false positives to gate deploys on. Calibrate, then set thresholds accordingly.</p>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ── §04 The Eval Flywheel ─────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-flywheel" data-code="flywheel">
<div class="section-no">04 — The Eval Flywheel</div>
<h2>Closing the Loop from Production to Test Suite</h2>

<p>The mistake teams make is treating eval as a one-time setup task. In reality, it's a continuous loop: production surfaces failures you didn't anticipate, those failures become test cases, test cases catch the next set of failures, and the system gets progressively more robust.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Eval flywheel — click a node to see what happens at each stage</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">interactive</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="flywheel-canvas" height="320" style="display:block;width:100%"></canvas>
    <div id="flywheel-detail" style="margin-top:.875rem;min-height:56px;font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted2);font-family:var(--ff-mono);line-height:1.6;background:var(--card2);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:6px;padding:.65rem .875rem;transition:all .2s">Click any node in the flywheel to see what happens at that stage →</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The flywheel has six stages, and all six must turn for it to compound:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Deploy &amp; Monitor</strong> — log every request, response, latency, and user feedback signal (thumbs up/down, re-prompts, session abandonment)</li>
  <li><strong>Surface Failures</strong> — sample intelligently: low user ratings, high latency, safety filter triggers, explicit re-prompts. Don't sample uniformly — failures are rare and need overrepresentation</li>
  <li><strong>Label &amp; Add to Dataset</strong> — have a human (or judge LLM) confirm it's a real failure, then add to the golden dataset. Track which version caused it</li>
  <li><strong>Run Offline Evals</strong> — run the full golden dataset through the current and candidate versions. Generate scores on all metrics</li>
  <li><strong>Fix the Issue</strong> — prompt engineering, RAG tuning, fine-tuning, guardrails, fallback logic — whatever it takes</li>
  <li><strong>Gate the Deploy</strong> — candidate must match or beat baseline on every tracked metric. Regressions on any metric block the deploy, not just the failing metric</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout callout-cyan">
  <div class="callout-title">🔄 The Flywheel Compounds</div>
  <p>A strong eval system creates two compounding benefits for free: your golden dataset <em>is</em> fine-tuning data, and your eval traces <em>are</em> a debugging database. Teams that invest in evals early ship faster later — not because they write fewer bugs, but because they find and fix them faster.</p>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ── §05 What to Measure ───────────────────────────────────── -->
