<h3>The metric that matters most: faithfulness for RAG</h3>
<p>Among all RAG metrics, <strong>faithfulness</strong> — does the answer stick to the retrieved context, or does it hallucinate beyond it? — is the single best predictor of user trust. A 2024 telecom domain study found faithfulness and factual correctness together align best with human expert judgment, while answer relevancy (cosine similarity-based) was "not very interpretable" and unreliable for specialized domains.</p>

<h3>Development-production skew</h3>
<p>One of the subtlest failure modes: your golden dataset represents the distribution at development time. Production traffic drifts — different vocabulary, different intents, different context lengths. Track two simple signals as early warnings:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Input length distribution</strong> — a sudden increase in median length often means users have found a complex use case you didn't test</li>
  <li><strong>Re-prompt rate</strong> — if users immediately re-ask the same question, your answer was unhelpful. This is free implicit feedback</li>
</ul>
</section>

