<h3>Key design choices</h3>
<p>A few things the reference pipeline gets right that most teams skip:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Absolute thresholds, not just relative baselines</strong> — if your baseline has silently degraded over three months, "beat the baseline" still lets you ship garbage. Hard minimums prevent threshold drift.</li>
  <li><strong>Canary before full deploy</strong> — LLM behavior in production is often subtly different from your golden dataset. A 5% canary catches distribution mismatches before they affect all users.</li>
  <li><strong>Versioned golden datasets</strong> — tag the dataset when you deploy a version. If a regression appears six months later, you can replay the exact eval from deployment day to understand what changed.</li>
  <li><strong>Failure-biased sampling</strong> — uniform sampling of production traffic wastes reviewer time. Oversample low-rated responses, re-prompts, and safety filter triggers — that's where your next test cases live.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout callout-green">
  <div class="callout-title">🚀 Where to Start</div>
  <p>Don't try to implement all five stages at once. Start with Stage 1 (assertion evals, takes an afternoon) and Stage 5 (sampling production traffic into a review queue). The two ends of the flywheel give you immediate visibility. Add Stage 2 once you have 50+ examples labeled. Stages 3 and 4 follow naturally from that baseline.</p>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ── §08 Agent CI/CD Tools ─────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-agents" data-code="agents">
<div class="section-no">08 — Agent CI/CD Tools</div>
<h2>Products Built for Multi-Step Agent Pipelines</h2>

<p>Evaluating a single LLM call is hard. Evaluating an agent — a system that makes dozens of sequential, branching tool calls before returning an answer — is a fundamentally harder problem. The same prompt can yield the right final answer through three completely different trajectories: one optimal, one wasteful, one that hallucinated and got lucky.</p>

<p>This is why a new category of tooling has emerged specifically for agent CI/CD. The core challenge has three dimensions that generic LLM eval tools weren't designed for:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Trajectory correctness</strong> — did the agent use the <em>right</em> tools in the <em>right</em> sequence? An agent that books a flight by first checking hotel availability has the wrong plan, even if it eventually books correctly.</li>
  <li><strong>Tool call fidelity</strong> — correct tool name, correct arguments, correct handling of errors. A single malformed API argument can silently corrupt downstream steps.</li>
  <li><strong>Compute budget</strong> — one agent run costs 10–1000× a single LLM call. You can run 10,000 unit evals per dollar; you might afford 10 full agent trajectories per dollar. Your test suite must be selective.</li>
</ul>

