<h3>The trajectory eval challenge</h3>
<p>A correct final answer is not sufficient evidence that the agent behaved correctly. You need to evaluate the <em>path</em> — and that path is different every run. Three techniques handle this:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Exact trajectory matching</strong> — compare tool call sequence against a golden reference. Catches wrong-order or extra steps. Brittle for complex tasks where multiple valid paths exist.</li>
  <li><strong>Tool-call subset checking</strong> — assert that a required set of tool calls were made, without enforcing order. "Did you call <code>search_db</code> at least once with the right entity?" Better for tasks with multiple valid paths.</li>
  <li><strong>LLM trajectory judge</strong> — show the judge the full sequence of steps and ask: "Was this the most efficient and correct way to complete this task?" Flexible, catches semantic inefficiencies, but more expensive.</li>
</ul>

